WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.772 --> 00:00:03.290
BALAJI SIVAGANESAN: Have you
ever wondered of loading data

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:06.770
from Cloud Storage to Bigtable
using the power of Spark

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:08.360
by just passing some arguments?

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.240
Stay tuned.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.070
This is Balaji.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.260
I'm a Cloud Migration
Consultant with Google Cloud.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.360
Dataproc templates
are an effort to solve

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.510
simple but large inflow
data tasks including

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.150
data import, export,
backup, restore,

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.500
and bulk AP operations.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.300
The technology under
the hood, which

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.060
makes these operations
possible is the serverless part

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.670
functionality based on
Google Cloud's data hub.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.770
Without having to create
them from scratch,

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.890
we can run typical use
cases on dataproc serverless

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.770
using Java and Python,
thanks to dataproc templates.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.180
With the help of
these templates,

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.940
we can simply customize
under common Spark workloads.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.120
Now we'll focus on GCS to
Bigtable Java template.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.080
This template is written in
Spark which is open source

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.390
and can be customizable based on
the requirements from the user.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.150
These are driven
by configurations

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:54.710
and are ready to use.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.260
This offers the user
the power of Spark

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.920
and customizing for advanced
use cases is super cool.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.960
One of the common
[INAUDIBLE] tasks

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.990
is to load data from
GCS to a low latency

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:10.970
database like Bigtable which
can power up any UI or real time

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.120
query platforms.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.760
This template supports CSV,
Parquet, and Avro file formats.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.580
These templates are ready
to use powered workloads,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.480
and they can be altered with
connection parameters for use

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.340
cases with identical
requirements

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.890
and the development can
be completed in no time.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.630
Here's the flow
of data in the GCS

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.190
to Bigtable dataproc template.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.500
The source is the
GCS storage, which

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.980
holds the file to be loaded into
Bigtable in a storage bucket

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.390
inside a specific folder.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.730
As of now, the template
supports loading

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.470
of CSV packet and out of files.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.250
The file has to be
uploaded in a folder,

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:49.400
and the exact location has
to be passed as an argument.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.190
Unlike other storage things,
Bigtable is quite different.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.340
It is known for its low
latency performance,

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.440
and the overall
query performance

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:58.760
depends on the row key.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.160
The data is stored as
column or storage format,

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.620
and each row has
its unique row key.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:07.520
The beauty of this NoSQL lies
in its columnar axis of data.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.789
So each column can alone be
accessed with its row key,

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:13.088
column family, and column name.

00:02:13.088 --> 00:02:14.630
[INAUDIBLE] the goal
of this template

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.600
lies the power of Spark
provided by dataproc serverless.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.490
It accesses the data in GCS and
loads the data in a [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:24.590
at a much faster
pace as it's driven

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.530
by the number of workloads
which are handled internally

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.000
by dataproc serverless.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.970
Once the batch job is
started, the data serverless

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:33.800
looks into the
job configuration,

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.590
and its starts
the Spark session.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:39.830
Right now there is no logic
written for any transformation.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.680
It just loads the data
into big tables from GCS.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.620
But in case of any
enhancements required,

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.630
it can be carried
out in no time.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.000
Here is a sample
CSV file and how

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.500
it gets loaded into Bigtable.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.240
As the row key
needs to be unique,

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.240
the first column in the CSV
file is taken as the row key,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.260
and it gets loaded.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.170
Further, the header details
are taken automatically

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:01.040
as the schema.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.080
The column family
is specifically

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.250
passed as an argument.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.590
As we see, the
various values gets

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.320
loaded as columns under
the given column family,

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:13.340
and the first column of the
given row is the row key.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.650
Here is a sample
Avro file and how

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.150
it gets loaded into Bigtable.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:21.620
From the input, we can see the
schema has a timestamp, tweet,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:22.550
and the user name.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.220
Since the username is the
first level value in the input,

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:26.510
that's taken as the row key.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.210
The various values
gets loaded as columns

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:30.680
under the given column family.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.610
The column names gets populated
automatically from the schema.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:37.010
Here is a sample
packet file and how

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:38.450
it gets loaded into Bigtable.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.570
From the input, we can see
the schema has continent

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:42.900
and the country array.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.540
The continent is
the first value,

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.250
and it gets loaded
as the row key.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.640
And the array of countries
gets loaded as a single value.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.860
Further, there are two
rows with the same row key.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.010
So it gets loaded
as two separate set

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:59.360
of columns under the same row
key, as it has to be unique.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.980
In the previous
video, you would have

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.960
seen it's a three-step
process to execute.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:06.470
First, the clone the repository
get authentication credentials,

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:07.890
and to execute the template.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.740
Now let's focus
on the third part,

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.210
and how to execute the template.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:14.420
First, create the GCS bucket,
then the input folder,

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.940
and upload the required
file into that folder.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:19.370
Doing this in a console
is the most preferred one.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.930
Next, create the
Bigtable instance

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.410
if it's already not
available in the cluster.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:27.260
Then within the instance,
create the Bigtable table

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.790
with the required column family.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:32.180
This column family has to
be passed in the arguments.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:35.660
Set the configuration
parameters like the GCP project,

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.870
region of execution, and
the GCP staging location

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:42.650
for internal use to upload the
jars and temporary storage.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:46.370
Then pass the arguments
and execute the template.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.640
This is to Bigtable Java,
post which the batch Spark

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.110
job has to be monitored.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.150
Here are the list
of arguments that

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:55.400
needs to be passed while
executing the template.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.880
The major ones are the location
of the input file, the details

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.940
of the Bigtable instance, the
table name, and the column

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:02.450
family.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:05.960
The respective project
tallies of the GCS storage

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.390
and the Bigtable also
needs to be passed.

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:11.030
Here are the commands used
to execute the template.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.880
The ones in red shows the
configuration parameters

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.490
that needs to be set one
time during the session.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.440
The start script will
trigger the template,

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.290
and the name of the template
has to be given along

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:23.060
with the required arguments.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.930
Once this is submitted, it
builds the jar, copies to GCS,

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.870
and then triggers a batch job
in the dataproc serverless.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:32.500
In this section, we will
cover hands-on on the GCS

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:33.560
to Bigtable template.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.990
First, we will start
with the Cloud Shell,

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.640
and after
authentication is done,

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.920
let's clone the dataproc
template repository

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.470
into the Cloud Shell.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:44.780
Then let's traverse
into the folder,

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.150
check the branch details
using the key status,

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.330
ensure that we are
in the main folder.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:52.640
Let's ensure that we
have the required input

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.220
files in the input
folder of the GCS packet.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:59.150
For instance, let's take a
CSV, Avro, and packet file.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:03.020
Let's see how the input CSV
file us-states2 looks like.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.030
It is a header and some record.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.330
So we can enter into
the Bigtable instance

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.450
in a Bigtable, and let's create
a table if it doesn't exist.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:14.130
For now, use the table bus-data.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.680
Please note that
the column family

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.920
needs to be given correctly
while creating the table.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:23.600
Now let's see the contents
of the bus data table.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.490
You can see there is
no data in the table.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.650
You would proceed with the
configuration parameters

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:31.130
to set up the configuration
parameters like GCP project,

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:33.810
region, GCP staging location.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.230
Once the basic
setup is done, next

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:38.630
is our template
specific processes.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.330
Populate the required documents
for running the template

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.870
like the location of
the input file in GCS,

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:48.260
expand, then the details on
the Bigtable table instance,

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:49.430
and the column family.

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.310
Copy the whole command for
executing the template.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:55.820
And the logs of the Spark
job execution are shown here.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:58.190
Once the job gets
completed, it clearly

00:06:58.190 --> 00:06:59.810
provides the details
of the completed

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:02.100
job along with the
additional details.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.850
The data from UI
shows the logs along

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.353
with the number of
executables that

00:07:06.353 --> 00:07:07.520
are running in the back end.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.730
In case, if we need to
view the detailed worker

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:14.750
level logs from Spark, it can be
seen from the view log section.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:18.200
Now let's check how the data
were loaded into the Bigtable.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.600
In our input we have
two records that we

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:24.440
can see that two records got
inserted into the Bigtable.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.280
As shown in the
example previously,

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.030
we can see the schema got
updated into the column

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:30.020
names automatically.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.870
With this, we are coming to
an end for the demo of GCS

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:34.100
to Bigtable template.

